{"checks":[{"id":"scope","question":"Search for related code that handles the same concept. Is the reported issue a symptom of something broader — and is any single step sized to fit one worker turn?","guidance":"Look at how the changed function is used across the codebase. Does the fix only address one caller's scenario while others remain broken? Flag missing required work or out-of-scope edits. A minimal patch is often right, but check whether the underlying problem is bigger than what the issue describes. Also audit step SIZING: each step/task is executed in ONE worker turn (~15 min, one conversation) and must be fully implemented AND verified there. A single step that bundles multiple concerns — e.g. 'extract this 2000-line file into modules AND write tests', or 'consolidate the roots AND migrate N registries AND add parity tests' — is a god-task: it cannot finish in one turn and the worker will run out of time mid-implementation. Raising complexity does not buy more time, only a stronger model for the same single turn. Flag any such step and recommend splitting along the natural seam (one step to add the abstraction + its unit tests, then one step per consumer/module to migrate, parity tests on the last).","findings":[{"detail":"Step 3 is ordered before the abstractions it says it will compose: the repository currently has only `compose_standard_bridge` in `astrid/packs/__init__.py`, which wires `ProjectRepository`, `TimelineRepository`, and the repository-based `TimelineBridgeAdapter`; the seven typed SDK service modules do not exist until Steps 4-8, and the bridge does not move onto services until Step 10. A worker cannot finish and verify a standard application containing typed services and prove SDK/bridge sharing in Step 3 without either implementing later steps early or leaving a knowingly incomplete composition. Split this into a writer/registry/repository/EventRepository composition step, a post-Steps-4-8 service-wiring step, and the bridge wiring proof with Step 10.","flagged":true},{"detail":"Steps 6-8 are each larger than one worker turn even though they are scored 4. The existing mutation surfaces are already large (`media.py` 2,024 lines, `runs.py` 2,342, `tasks.py` 4,279, references repository 2,168, shots repository 1,527), while those steps add new event-backed mutations/read models, two-service orchestration in Steps 7 and 8, idempotency/error envelopes, and broad conformance across existing test files totaling thousands of lines. Split Step 6 into repository-backed verify, single-file service operations, and directory batching/relations; split Step 7 into generic run/event reads, task service, and run service; and split Step 8 into reference-update repository work, reference service, and shot service, with public conformance after each seam.","flagged":true},{"detail":"Step 9 is also a god-task on the actual SDK surface: `tests/test_sdk_public_surface.py` is 3,391 lines and contains many monkeypatches of `astrid.sdk.run_executor`/`run_orchestrator`, while `invoke` currently calls those public seams. Adding a seven-service context-managed client, preserving lazy capability/render/event behavior, internalizing the runner seams, curating both `astrid.sdk` and top-level exports, and migrating that regression suite cannot be safely completed and verified in one turn. Split internal runner-seam migration plus invoke regression from `AstridClient` lifecycle/service exposure, then make public-export/lazy-import reduction a final step.","flagged":true},{"detail":"Step 11 omits a required live caller and combines three independent high-risk changes. `create_local_bridge_server` is called by `_dispatch_serve` in `astrid/core/gateway/dispatch.py`, which currently constructs the server without a bridge and assigns `server.bridge` afterward; requiring injection at construction will break that path unless the gateway is migrated in the same step, not deferred to the unrelated product-registry work in Step 13. The 979-line server and 2,131-line server test file also make constructor/fallback removal, persisted media-alias plus SHA-256 authorization, and Range/ETag/CORS streaming consolidation too much for one turn. Split constructor injection (including gateway and constructor tests), repository-backed asset authorization, and HTTP streaming semantics into separate verified steps.","flagged":true},{"detail":"The CLI cutover in Steps 13-16 is broader than adding thin parsers. The existing gateway routes `projects` to a 631-line legacy CLI, `timelines` to a parser module of 1,625 lines, and `runs` through lifecycle-engine extraction, file-backed audit/gc verbs, the deprecated singular alias, `LIFECYCLE_VERBS`, session gating, and root help. Existing alias/gateway/public-surface tests that encode those paths exceed 6,000 lines in the directly relevant files. Split the registry/renderer abstraction from one-family-at-a-time mounts, and add explicit gateway cutover steps for projects/timelines/runs that update lifecycle/session allowlists, help, alias removal, and the existing regression tests; otherwise a single worker for Step 13 or Step 16 must both build a new product surface and unwind several legacy authorities.","flagged":true},{"detail":"Step 17 fixes only the central `astrid/core/util/secrets.py` path, but the same broad env-file discovery is reintroduced in `astrid/packs/rendering/executors/sprite_sheet/upscale.py`, whose `_workspace_env_files` explicitly adds cwd, workspace, and sibling Reigh `.env` files after calling the shared helper. Related tests also currently assert env-file-over-process-environment behavior (for example `tests/packs/test_generate_image_openai.py`), so changing only the listed central tests leaves either a policy bypass or full-suite failures. Expand the credential-policy migration to all direct env walkers and affected caller tests, and split that migration/keychain adapter from the separate all-domain zero-secret journey and persisted-secret scan.","flagged":true}]}],"flags":[{"id":"CF-SCOPE-GOD-TASKS","concern":"Multiple plan steps bundle repository behavior, new service APIs, consumer cutovers, and multi-thousand-line regression verification beyond one worker turn.","category":"completeness","severity_hint":"high","evidence":"Steps 6-9 and 11, plus the Step 13-16 CLI cutover, span existing 1,000-4,000-line implementation modules and similarly large test surfaces; the natural repository/service/consumer seams are not separate tasks."},{"id":"CF-SCOPE-MISSING-CALLERS","concern":"The bridge-injection and credential-precedence work does not enumerate all existing callers implementing the same concepts.","category":"completeness","severity_hint":"high","evidence":"`astrid/core/gateway/dispatch.py::_dispatch_serve` uses post-construction bridge injection, and `astrid/packs/rendering/executors/sprite_sheet/upscale.py::_workspace_env_files` independently adds broad env-file candidates."}],"verified_flag_ids":[],"disputed_flag_ids":[]}